**Read:** [Applied Mechanics of Solids — Allan F. Bower](../../mechanical-engineering-and-machine-design/sources/Applied-Mechanics-of-Solids-Bower.lingua#r_109WFZBWMEMGHV9TGFD22H2F1W), constitutive and failure chapters; [Mechanical Behavior of Materials — Krystyn Van Vliet, MIT 3.22](../../../materials/metals-and-metallurgical-production/sources/Mechanical-Behavior-MIT-3-22.lingua#r_53J3ZM414SJR3SFW7Q1G8ZJWDC), special-topic project guidance; [Wood Handbook — Forest Products Laboratory, 2021](../sources/Wood-Handbook-FPL-2021.lingua#r_2SP2KMHCD78MQGZN69GKPGJ3WM), chapters 5 and 7; [Breviary Technical Ceramics — Verband der Keramischen Industrie](../sources/Breviary-Technical-Ceramics.lingua#r_7998JE61RKQF7VXXYK197KRBH4), quality and design discussions.

Homogenization replaces a spatially varying body with an effective description for a stated question and scale. The representative volume must be large enough to sample relevant heterogeneity, yet small enough compared with the macroscopic variation being modeled. Damage, rare flaws and long-range correlations can defeat a simple representative-volume assumption.

A microscopic modulus, a coupon curve and a component response are related through boundary conditions, structure and uncertainty. Processing texture, surface layers, joining zones and residual stress can make a single bulk property inadequate. Evidence should follow the quantities that the actual prediction needs.

**Work:** Trace one microstructure-to-performance claim across at least three scales. Identify the equations and information discarded at each transition. Compare a mean response with a lower-tail failure question, and explain why agreeing with one does not verify the other. Carry the missing information into the advanced studies.

